 construct:{q:"Whether the children of unbelievers ought to be baptized against their parents' will?",ref:"ST II-II q.10 a.12",
  model:{obj:"It seems so: the bond of marriage is broken by unbelief; much more then the bond between parent and child; and children belong more to God than to parents.",sc:"Injustice should be done to no one; it would be unjust to take children from parents' care against their will.",resp:"The custom of the Church never was to baptize the children of unbelievers against their parents' will, for two reasons: danger to the faith (children might later be led to reject it), and natural justice. A child is by nature part of its father; before birth enfolded in the womb, and after birth, before the use of free will, enfolded in the care of its parents, 'like a spiritual womb'. It would be against natural justice to remove a child from that care, or to do anything against the parents' will, before the child has the use of reason.",rep:"Man is directed to God by reason; before the age of reason, the child is directed to God by the reason of its parents."},
  rubric:["Objection: children belong more to God than parents.","Sed contra: injustice to no one.","Respondeo: Church custom; danger to faith.","Respondeo: natural justice; the spiritual womb.","Application: parents' primary right and duty in formation."]},
 drill:{title:"Order a positive-discipline response to a tantrum",parts:["Stay calm; regulate yourself","Ensure safety","Ignore minor attention-seeking if safe","Apply a calm logical consequence if needed","Reconnect when calm","Praise the next instance of good behaviour"]},
