 recall:[
  ["p4l2","What are common factors?","Alliance, empathy, expectations, a credible rationale and healing actions shared by effective therapies."],
  ["p12","Which survey gives SA's lifetime prevalence of mental disorders?","SASH (Herman et al., 2009): 30.3%."],
  ["p17","What share of people with bipolar spectrum disorder in low-income countries reached mental health services (Merikangas et al., 2011)?","25.2%."],
  ["t10","What is subsidiarity?","Higher communities should not absorb the functions of lower ones, but support them (CCC 1883)."]],
 reading:{primary:[["Chibanda et al. (2016) the Friendship Bench, JAMA 316(24), 2618–2626",'https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.19102'],["Mental Health Care Act 17 of 2002",'https://www.gov.za/documents/mental-health-care-act'],["ST II-II q.32 a.2 (corporal and spiritual almsdeeds)",NA("3032.htm#article2")]],
  secondary:["Patel, V. et al. (2018) 'The Lancet Commission on global mental health and sustainable development', The Lancet, 392(10157), pp. 1553–1598.","Petersen, I. et al. (2016) 'Strengthening mental health system governance in six low- and middle-income countries in Africa and South Asia', International Journal of Mental Health Systems, 10, 9.","Docrat, S. et al. (2019) 'Mental health system costs, resources and constraints in South Africa: a national survey', Health Policy and Planning, 34(9), pp. 706–719.","Francis (2015) Laudato Si' and (2020) Fratelli Tutti, ch. 2 (the Good Samaritan)."]},
 scripture:[["Matthew 25:35–40, as long as you did it to one of these my least brethren",null],["Acts 2:44–45, they had all things common",null],["Luke 10:36–37, go, and do thou in like manner",null]],
 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. The treatment gap",html:`<p>Most South Africans with a mental disorder receive no treatment. Reasons include: few specialists, concentrated in cities and the private sector; a small share of the health budget for mental health; stigma; language and cultural barriers; poverty, transport and time costs; and competing explanations (spiritual, ancestral). Public services rely heavily on psychiatric hospitals and primary-care nurses.</p>
<p>The Life Esidimeni tragedy (2016), in which over 140 mental health care users died after transfer from a contracted facility to unlicensed NGOs in Gauteng, exposed how vulnerable these patients are; the Health Ombud's report and the subsequent arbitration found grave failures of governance.</p>
${RC("p12",`<p>P3 L1: <strong>SASH</strong> documented high prevalence; this lesson asks why so few receive care, and what can be done.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Which is a major cause of the treatment gap in SA?",[["Too many psychologists in rural areas","No."],["Few specialists concentrated in cities and private sector","Correct."],["Low prevalence","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Task-sharing: the Friendship Bench",html:`<div class="co core"><div class="co-h">[Core] Chibanda et al. (2016), JAMA 316(24), 2618–2626</div><p>Cluster RCT in 24 primary care clinics in Harare (N = 573; 86% women; 42% HIV-positive). Six sessions of problem-solving therapy by trained, supervised lay health workers ('grandmothers') on a bench, plus optional peer support, vs enhanced usual care. At 6 months: lower symptom scores (adjusted mean difference −4.86 on the SSQ-14) and lower risk of depression symptoms (13.7% vs 49.9%). doi:10.1001/jama.2016.19102.</p></div>
<p>Task-sharing works when non-specialists are <strong>well trained, supervised and supported</strong>, using structured, simple interventions (problem-solving, behavioural activation, interpersonal counselling), with clear referral pathways for severe cases. South African studies (e.g. the PRIME programme; registered counsellors and lay counsellors in NGOs) apply similar models.</p>
${RC("p4l2",`<p>P4 L2: <strong>common factors</strong> help explain why trained lay workers can be effective: warmth, a credible plan, and structured action.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("In the Friendship Bench trial, who delivered the therapy?",[["Psychiatrists","No."],["Trained, supervised lay health workers","Correct."],["Priests","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. Policy and law",html:`<ul><li><strong>Mental Health Care Act 17 of 2002</strong>: rights of mental health care users; voluntary, assisted and involuntary care; review boards; integration into general health care.</li>
<li><strong>National Mental Health Policy Framework and Strategic Plan</strong> (the Department of Health's current plan): community-based care, integration into primary care, task-sharing, and mental health promotion. Check the Department of Health's website for the current version.</li>
<li><strong>Health Professions Act</strong> and HPCSA categories: clinical, counselling, educational, research and industrial psychologists; registered counsellors (a category intended for primary-level care; scope revisions have been contested); psychometrists.</li></ul>
${RC("p4l3",`<p>P4 L3: the <strong>law and ethics</strong> framework applies also to lay counsellors and parish ministries: confidentiality, consent and referral.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Which Act governs voluntary, assisted and involuntary mental health care?",[["Mental Health Care Act 17 of 2002","Correct."],["Children's Act","No."],["POPIA","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. The works of mercy and subsidiarity",html:`<p>Thomas derives the spiritual works of mercy from human needs: prayer, and human help for deficiencies of the intellect (instructing, counselling), of the appetite \"especially by way of sorrow, which is remedied by 'comforting'\", and of inordinate acts (reproof, forgiving, bearing wrongs) (II-II q.32 a.2). The corporal works include visiting the sick and burying the dead.</p>
<p>A parish is an ideal place for these works: it is local, trusted, and present across a lifetime. <strong>Subsidiarity</strong> (CCC 1883) suggests that families and parishes should not be replaced by professionals, but supported by them; and professionals should not be replaced by well-meaning amateurs where specialist care is needed.</p>
${RC("t10",`<p>T10: Catholic social teaching's <strong>subsidiarity</strong> and solidarity frame a mixed system of care: family, parish, NGO, clinic and hospital each doing what it can do best.</p>`)}`,
  check:Q("Comforting the sorrowful remedies, for Thomas, a deficiency of…",[["the intellect","That is instructing/counselling."],["the appetite, especially by way of sorrow","Correct: II-II q.32 a.2."],["the body","Corporal works."]],1)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. Designing a parish mental-health ministry",html:`<ol><li><strong>Purpose</strong>: welcome, support, education and referral, not therapy.</li>
<li><strong>Team</strong>: clergy, any professionals in the parish, trained volunteers; clear supervision.</li>
<li><strong>Training</strong>: mental health first aid; listening skills; suicide-risk response; boundaries and confidentiality.</li>
<li><strong>Referral map</strong>: local clinics, hospital emergency units, SADAG, SANCA, university counselling, private practitioners with sliding scales.</li>
<li><strong>Groups</strong>: bereavement, carers of people with mental illness, recovery (AA/NA meetings in the hall).</li>
<li><strong>Liturgy and teaching</strong>: homilies that name mental illness without shame; Anointing of the Sick for serious mental illness; prayers for those who died by suicide.</li>
<li><strong>Safeguards</strong>: no deliverance or exorcism outside diocesan norms; no advice to stop medication.</li></ol>
<div class="co case"><div class="co-h">Case · a Mamelodi parish</div><p>A parish starts a monthly 'Bench' after Sunday Mass, staffed by two trained volunteers supervised by a registered counsellor, with a referral list and a crisis protocol. In its first year it supports 60 people, refers 12 to the clinic, and hosts a carers' group for families of people with schizophrenia.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("What should a parish mental-health ministry NOT do?",[["Refer to clinics","It should."],["Advise people to stop medication","Correct: never."],["Offer bereavement groups","It can."]],1)}
 ],
